NNAAMMEE
     ttrruunnkk — link aggregation and link failover interface

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ppsseeuuddoo--ddeevviiccee ttrruunnkk

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ttrruunnkk interface allows aggregation of multiple network interfaces as
     one virtual ttrruunnkk interface.

     A ttrruunnkk interface can be created using the iiffccoonnffiigg ttrruunnkk_N ccrreeaattee com‐
     mand.

     The driver currently supports the trunk protocols bbrrooaaddccaasstt, ffaaiilloovveerr,
     llaaccpp, llooaaddbbaallaannccee, nnoonnee, and rroouunnddrroobbiinn (the default).  The protocols de‐
     termine which ports are used for outgoing traffic and whether a specific
     port accepts incoming traffic.  The interface link state is used to vali‐
     date if the port is active or not.

     bbrrooaaddccaasstt    Sends frames to all ports of the trunk and receives frames
                  on any port of the trunk.

     ffaaiilloovveerr     Sends and receives traffic only through the master port.  If
                  the master port becomes unavailable, the next active port is
                  used.  The first interface added is the master port; any in‐
                  terfaces added after that are used as failover devices.

     llaaccpp         Uses the IEEE 802.3ad (renamed to 802.1AX in 2014) Link Ag‐
                  gregation Control Protocol (LACP) and the Marker Protocol to
                  increase link speed and provide redundancy.  LACP trunk
                  groups are composed of ports of the same speed, set to full-
                  duplex operation.  This protocol requires a switch which
                  supports LACP.  By default, the LACP implementation uses ac‐
                  tive-mode LACP, slow timeout, and 0x8000 (medium) priority
                  as system and port priorities.

     llooaaddbbaallaannccee  Distributes outgoing traffic through all active ports and
                  accepts incoming traffic from any active port.  A hash of
                  the protocol header is used to maintain packet ordering.
                  The hash includes the Ethernet source and destination ad‐
                  dress and, if available, the VLAN tag, and the IP source and
                  destination address.

     nnoonnee         This protocol is intended to do nothing: it disables any
                  traffic without disabling the ttrruunnkk interface itself.

     rroouunnddrroobbiinn   Distributes outgoing traffic through all active ports and
                  accepts incoming traffic from any active port.  A round-
                  robin scheduler is used to aggregate the traffic.

     The configuration can be done at runtime or by setting up a
     hostname.if(5) configuration file for netstart(8).

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Create a simple round robin trunk with two bge(4) Gigabit Ethernet inter‐
     faces:

           # ifconfig bge0 up
           # ifconfig bge1 up
           # ifconfig trunk0 trunkport bge0 trunkport bge1 \
                   192.168.1.1 netmask 255.255.255.0

     The following example uses an active failover trunk to set up roaming be‐
     tween wired and wireless networks using two network devices.  Whenever
     the wired master interface is unplugged, the wireless failover device
     will be used:

           # ifconfig em0 up
           # ifconfig ath0 nwid my_net up
           # ifconfig trunk0 trunkproto failover trunkport em0 trunkport ath0 \
                   192.168.1.1 netmask 255.255.255.0

